(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["interpretation-of-medical-literature-book-questions-chapter-11-chance-quiz"]={"title":"Chapter 11: Chance Quiz — Book Questions — Interpretation of Medical Literature","category":"Interpretation of Medical Literature","exam":"Book Questions","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A randomized controlled trial of thrombolytic therapy versus angioplasty for acute myocardial infarction finds no difference in the main outcome, survival to discharge from hospital. The investigators explored whether this was also true for subgroups of patients defined by age, number of vessels affected, ejection fraction, comorbidity, and other patient characteristics. Which of the following is not true about this subgroup analysis?","choices":["Examining subgroups increases the chance of a false-positive (misleading statistically significant) result in one of the comparisons.","Examining subgroups increases the chance of a false-negative finding in one of these subgroups, relative to the main result.","Subgroup analyses are bad scientific practice and should not be done.","Reporting results in subgroups helps clinicians tailor information in the study to individual patients."],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. This is the statement that is not true. Subgroup analyses carry risks of both false-positive and false-negative conclusions, but with those limits in mind (ideally with the subgroups chosen beforehand and the number of comparisons reported) they give useful information, so they should not be banned. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 17; 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 18; 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 11; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 11.1"},{"q":"A new drug for hyperlipidemia was compared with placebo in a randomized controlled trial of 10,000 patients. After 2 years, serum cholesterol (the primary outcome) was 238 mg/dL in the group receiving the new drug and 240 mg/dL in the group receiving the old drug (P <0.001). Which of the following best describes the meaning of the P value in this study?","choices":["Bias is unlikely to account for the observed difference.","The difference is clinically important.","A difference as big as or bigger than what was observed could have arisen by chance less than one time in 1,000.","The results are generalizable to other patients with hypertension.","The statistical power of this study was inadequate."],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The P value is the probability of seeing a difference this large or larger by chance alone; P < 0.001 means that chance would produce it less than 1 time in 1,000. It says nothing about bias, importance, or generalizability. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 7; 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 22; 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 34; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 11.2"},{"q":"In a well-designed clinical trial of treatment for ovarian cancer, remission rate at 1 year is 30% in patients offered a new drug and 20% in those offered a placebo. The P value is 0.4. Which of the following best describes the interpretation of this result?","choices":["Both treatments are effective.","Neither treatment is effective.","The statistical power of this study is 60%.","The best estimate of treatment effect size is 0.4.","There is insufficient information to decide whether one treatment is better than the other."],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. The P value of 0.4 is far above 0.05, so an effect is not established, but it also cannot exclude a real, clinically important effect that a study with too little power missed. The evidence is inconclusive in either direction. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 7; 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 11; 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 22; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 11.3"},{"q":"In a cohort study, vitamin A intake was found to be a risk factor for hip fracture in women. The relative risk (highest quintile vs. lowest quintile) was 1.48, and the 95% confidence interval was 1.05–2.07. Which of the following best describes the meaning of this confidence interval?","choices":["The association is not statistically significant at the P <0.05 level.","A strong association between vitamin A intake and hip fracture was established.","The statistical power of this study is 95%.","There is a 95% chance that a range of relative risks as low as 1.05 and as high as 2.07 includes the true risk.","Bias is an unlikely explanation for this result."],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A 95 percent confidence interval is a range of values (here relative risks from 1.05 to 2.07) that should contain the true value, with 95 percent confidence. It shows the precision of the estimate. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 14; 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 15; 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 34; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 11.4"},{"q":"Which of the following is the best reason for calling P ≤0.05 “statistically significant?”","choices":["It definitively rules out a false-positive conclusion.","It is an arbitrarily chosen but useful rule of thumb.","It rules out a type II error.","It is a way of establishing a clinically important effect size.","Larger or smaller P values do not provide useful information."],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The 0.05 cutoff is an arbitrary number that researchers agreed on by convention. It is useful as a rule of thumb, but it has no special mathematical or clinical meaning. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 7; 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 22; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 11.5"},{"q":"Which of the following is the biggest advantage of multivariable modeling?","choices":["Models can control for many variables simultaneously.","Models do not depend on assumptions about the data.","There is a standardized and reproducible approach to modeling.","Models make stratified analyses unnecessary.","Models can control for confounding in large randomized controlled trials."],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Multivariable modeling examines the effect of two or more variables together, so it can take many other variables (confounders or effect modifiers) into account at the same time, which stratified analysis cannot do easily. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 19; 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 20; 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 21; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 11.6"},{"q":"A trial randomizes 30,000 patients to two treatment groups of similar size: one offered chemoprevention and the other usual care. How frequently must a side effect of chemoprevention occur for the study to have a good chance of observing at least one such side effect?","choices":["1/5,000","1/10,000","1/15,000","1/20,000","1/30,000"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Only about 15,000 people (half of 30,000) receive chemoprevention. The rule of thumb is that observing at least one event needs about 3 divided by the event rate in people; 3 divided by 15,000 is 1 in 5,000, which gives about a 95 percent chance of seeing at least one case. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 11.7"},{"q":"A noninferiority randomized trial compared arthroscopic surgery versus physical therapy for knee injury with meniscal tears. The primary outcome was improvement in knee function, using a scale of 0 to 100, with better function indicated by higher scores. The noninferiority margin was defined as an 8-point difference in improvement, and a one-sided α level was set at 0.025. At 2 years follow-up, the knee surgery group had an improvement in function of 3.6 greater than the physical therapy group, P = 0.001 for noninferiority. Based on the results, which conclusion is true?","choices":["There was no clinically important difference between surgery and physical therapy.","There was a statistically significant greater improvement in knee function for the surgery group compared to the physical therapy group.","The study could not reject the null hypothesis based on these findings.","The study may have erroneously concluded a true difference exists between surgery and physical therapy.","A smaller margin would have been more likely to be statistically significant."],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The margin of 8 points is the investigators' judgment of a clinically important difference. The observed difference of 3.6 is below it and the noninferiority test rejected the null hypothesis that physical therapy is worse, so no clinically important difference exists. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 29; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 11.8"},{"q":"Which of the following is least related to the statistical power of a study with a dichotomous outcome?","choices":["Effect size","Type I error","Rate of outcome events in the control group","Type II error","The statistical test used"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. The statistical test used can change the calculated power slightly but is not a main determinant. Power depends mainly on effect size, event rate, the type I error level and the type II error level, which together set sample size. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 9; 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 11; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 11.9"},{"q":"Investigators derive a model to predict postoperative mortality from data on a cohort of patients undergoing surgery for hip fractures. The final model includes variables for heart function (ejection fraction), kidney function (creatinine), and age. Asthma was assessed for inclusion in the model, but did not affect the prediction. The model was found to have excellent discrimination (sensitivity = 0.91, specificity = 0.93, c-statistic = 0.88) and internal calibration. Which of the following is true?","choices":["The positive predictive values can be calculated from the provided performance characteristics.","The model performance indicates that it would be useful for risk stratification.","Asthma is a confounder and thus excluded from the final model.","Age confounds the other model variables and could be removed from the prediction model.","Age, creatinine, and ejection fraction are risk factors for postoperative mortality in this study."],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. A prediction model keeps the variables that each improve prediction of the outcome, so age, creatinine and ejection fraction are risk factors for postoperative mortality in this study, and asthma is not. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 19; 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 26; 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 29; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 11.10"},{"q":"Which of the following best characterizes the application of Bayesian reasoning to a clinical trial?","choices":["Prior belief in the comparative effectiveness of treatment is guided by equipoise.","The results of each new study change belief in treatment effect from what it was before the study.","Bayesian inference is an alternative way of calculating a P value.","Bayesian reasoning is based, like inferential statistics, on the null hypothesis.","Bayesian reasoning depends on a well-defined hypothesis before the study is begun."],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bayesian reasoning starts from a prior belief about the treatment effect and updates it with each new study, so belief after the study differs from belief before it. The same idea appears when a test result changes pretest probability into post-test probability. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 33; 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 13; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 11.11"},{"q":"In a randomized trial of intensive glucose lowering in type 2 diabetes, death rate was higher in the intensively treated patients: hazard ratio 1.22 (95% confidence interval 1.01–1.46). Which of the following is not true about this study?","choices":["The results are consistent with almost no effect.","The best estimate of treatment effect is a hazard ratio of 1.22.","If a P value were calculated, the results would be statistically significant at the 0.05 level.","A P value would provide as much information as the confidence interval.","The results are consistent with 46% higher death rates in the intensively treated patients."],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. This statement is not true. A P value tells only whether chance is a plausible explanation. The interval also gives the point estimate and the range of effects consistent with the data, so it carries more information. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 14; 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 15; 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 7; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 11.12"}]};
